<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Solve Linear Equations: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00036</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Solve Linear Equations: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>edebdd5e-ce08-4ce6-9e3a-d6ef52690d42</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <note class="mini-lesson-question" id="note-00001">
        <title>Question #1: Solve Linear Equations</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Solve: \( 2(m - 6) + 3 = m - 1 \).</para>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Solving Linear Equations Using a General Strategy</title>
      <para id="para-00002">A solution of an equation is a value of a variable that makes a true statement when substituted into the equation. For example, in \( x + 2 = 3 \), the value of the variable \( x \) is 1.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">To find the solution to an equation in one variable, the goal is to isolate the variable on one side of the equation. For example,</para>
      <para id="para-00004">\( 3x + 1 = 9 - x \)</para>
      <para id="para-00005">\( +x             +x \)</para>
      <para id="para-00006">\( 4x + 1 = 9 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00007">      \( -1    -1 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00008">      \( 4x = 8 \)<newline/></para>
      <para id="para-00009">You can check the solution by substituting the value into the equation.<newline/></para>
      <para id="para-00010">\( 3(2) + 1 = 9 - 2 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00011">     \( 6 + 1 = 7 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00012">           \( 7 = 7 \)<newline/></para>
      <para id="para-00013">Equations may take several steps to solve, so it is helpful to have a clear and organized strategy. The following table shows a general strategy to solve any <emphasis>linear equation</emphasis> in one variable. You may not need to use every step.<newline/></para>
      <note id="note-00002">
        <!--BEGIN
        STEPS-->
        <title>General Strategy for Solving Linear Equations</title>
        <para id="para-00014"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis>
       </para>
          <para id="para-00015">Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Use the <emphasis>Distributive Property</emphasis> to remove any parentheses.</para>
          <para id="para-00017">Combine like <emphasis>terms</emphasis> .</para>
          <newline/>
          <para id="para-00018"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis>
       </para>
          <para id="para-00019">Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Use the <emphasis>Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality</emphasis>.</para>
          <newline/>
          <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis>
       </para>
          <para id="para-00022">Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
          <newline/>
          <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00025">Make the <emphasis>coefficient</emphasis> of the variable term to equal to 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">State the solution to the equation.</para>
          <newline/>
          <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00028">Check the solution.</para>
          <para id="para-00029">Substitute the solution into the original equation to make sure the result is a true statement.</para>
        <!-- END STEPS -->
      </note>
      <note class="try" id="note-00003">
        <title>Try It: Solving a Linear Equation Using a General Strategy</title>
          <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00030">Solve the following using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Solve \( 2(3x - 8) = 19 - x \).</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00033">Here is how to solve this linear equation, \( 2(3x - 8) = 19 - x \), using a general strategy:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="2">
                <tbody>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Distribute.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 2(3x - 8) = 19 - x \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Add \( x \) to get the variables on the left.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 6x + x - 16 = 19 - x + x \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 7x - 16 = 19 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Add 2 to get the constants on the right.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 7x - 16 + 16 = 19 + 16 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 7x = 35 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( 7x = 35 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Divide by 7 to make the coefficient of the variable equal to 1.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry>\( \frac{7x}{7} = \frac{35}{7} \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( x = 5 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Check.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( 2(3x - 8) = 19 - x \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Substitute 5 for \( x \).</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( 2(3(5) - 8) = 19 - (5) \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( 2(15 - 8) = 14 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( 2(7) = 14 \)</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>
                      <emphasis>Simplify.</emphasis>
                    </entry>
                    <entry> \( 14 = 14 \) True \(\checkmark\)</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
      <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
      <exercise id="bcbf3602-7944-41bc-8a75-3e5b36561481" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00034">Solve: \( 7(n - 3) - 8 = -15 \).</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para id="para-00036">\( n = - \frac{4}{7} \)</para>
          <para id="para-00037">\( n = -2 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00038">\( n = 2 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00039">\( n = -3 \frac{5}{7} \)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00040">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Use the Distributive Property to eliminate the parentheses. Be sure to multiply 7 by \( n \) and 7 by 3.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Videos</title>
